course: dictionary
lesson: 5622
title: Likeness: being alike
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A likeness is how much one thing looks like, or is like, another thing. You can see a likeness between two people, or between a real person and a picture of them. It is a noun, so you can say a likeness, or likenesses for more than one.
explain: Look for the clues around the word. «There's a strong likeness between you and your mum.» Strong tells you the likeness is big, so you look a lot alike. A family likeness is when relatives share the same nose, smile or eyes.
explain: A likeness can also be a picture, drawing or statue of a real person. In art lessons, a portrait that is a good likeness looks just like the person it shows. Try your meaning in the sentence: a good likeness of someone is a picture that looks like them, and that makes sense.
explain: Some words only look alike by chance. «Emoji» is Japanese, and its likeness to «emotion» is a coincidence. The two words look similar, but one did not come from the other, so the likeness is just luck.
fact: Likeness means being alike, or how much one thing looks like another.
fact: A likeness can also be a picture, drawing or statue that looks like a real person.
fact: «Likeness» is a noun: a likeness, likenesses.
fact: Useful phrases: a strong likeness, a family likeness, a good likeness.
exercise: «There's a strong likeness between you and your mum.» What does likeness mean here? | A loud noise | *Being alike, how much you look alike | A kind of food | Being rich and well off
exercise: A portrait that is a good likeness does what? | *Looks just like the person it shows | Is made only of stone | Is always in colour | Hides the face
exercise: Copy the phrase that means a picture that looks like a real person. | a long walk | a loud voice | a family dinner | *a good likeness
test: What does a family likeness show? | *Relatives who share features and look alike | A type of bread | A wet day in the park | A trip to the sea
test: Jun says a likeness is always a photo. Is Jun right? | Yes, only photos count | *No, it can also be a drawing, painting or statue | Yes, only real people can have one | No, a likeness is always a word
exam: «Emoji» is Japanese, and its likeness to «emotion» is a coincidence. What does likeness mean here? | Being in charge of the word | *Looking or sounding alike, only by chance | A type of Japanese food | A picture of the word
